--TEST--
`Core\IO::open` answers a `Core\IO\File` a declared type can name, its three members carry a whole round trip, and a handle used after `close` throws rather than reading another file — spec § 14 R14, `rule:security/capability-check-at-the-door`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\IO\File::close
// covers: Core\IO\File::read
// covers: Core\IO\File::write
// The declaration is the assertion. PHP's `fopen` answers a `resource`, which
// is an integer with a type tag every function taking one has to re-check at
// run time; here the type is checked before the program runs, and what is left
// for a body to ask is only whether the handle is still open.
Core\IO\File $out = Core\IO::open("note.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::Write);
uint $written = $out->write("hello world\n");
echo "wrote ", $written as string, "\n";
$out->close();

// The read half, through a second handle: without a `seek` the writing handle
// is at the end of what it wrote, and reading back is what proves the bytes
// reached the file rather than a buffer.
Core\IO\File $in = Core\IO::open("note.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::Read);
echo "first: ", $in->read(5), "\n";
echo "rest: ", $in->read(64);
// The one answer `read` gives that is neither data nor an error.
echo "eof: ", Core\Str::length($in->read(64)) as string, "\n";
$in->close();

// A closed handle is not a handle to something else. The key a `File` holds is
// never reused, so this can only ever be the same file twice, and it is a bug
// in the program rather than a state of the file -- which is why it is a
// `RuntimeError` and not an `IOError`.
try {
    $in->read(1);
    echo "not reached\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $e) {
    echo "after close: ", $e->message, "\n";
}

// And closing twice is the same mistake reported the same way.
try {
    $in->close();
    echo "not reached\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $e) {
    echo "closed twice: ", $e->message, "\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
write = ["."]
--EXPECTF--
wrote 12
first: hello
rest:  world
eof: 0
after close: Core\IO\File::read: this handle is closed — %s%enote.txt
closed twice: Core\IO\File::close: this handle is closed — %s%enote.txt
